fbthrift on darwin (macOS): (A) a Thrift server reply-latency fix, (B) build fixes for thrift/lib/python (the
thrift.python / thrift.py3 extension modules + libthrift_python_cpp, packaged
as the `thrift` wheel that edenfsctl.real's thrift-python bindings need).

Applied by getdeps via `patchfile = fbthrift-darwin.patch` in manifests/fbthrift AND
manifests/fbthrift-python (git apply --ignore-space-change from the fbthrift
checkout root; fbthrift and fbthrift-python share that checkout and the
`.getdeps_patched` sentinel). Upstream only ever built thrift_python on
Linux. Darwin-specific behaviour is guarded by if(APPLE) / sys.platform;
the rest is a no-op on Linux.

Hunks in thrift/lib/python/CMakeLists.txt:

1. folly .pxd lookup: add <folly prefix>/include as a HINT — folly's CMake
   installs the .pxd files there too (the site-packages hints stay first).

2. Libiberty is Linux-only: folly does not use it on darwin, the folly-python
   manifest does not provide it there, and the macOS libiberty builds lack
   the demanglers. LIBIBERTY_LIBRARIES is empty on APPLE.

3. Staging rename Sink.cpp -> SinkImpl.cpp and Sink.h -> SinkImpl.h in
   symlink_dir_contents: the --api-only pass writes streaming/sink.cpp and
   sink.h next to sink.pyx, which on a case-insensitive filesystem are the
   same paths as the staged handwritten Sink.cpp/Sink.h, and Cython refuses
   to overwrite files it did not generate ("The output file already exists
   and does not look like it was generated by Cython"). Nothing includes the
   staged copies by relative path (Sink.cpp/bidi_stream.cpp/sink.pxd use
   <thrift/lib/python/streaming/Sink.h> from the source tree).

4. SETUP_LDFLAGS: -Wl,--no-as-needed is GNU-ld only, so the darwin default is
   empty, and on APPLE the flags gain -Wl,-undefined,dynamic_lookup (the
   extension modules link only libthrift_python_cpp; folly/python symbols
   bind at load time, exactly like python's own LDSHARED does it),
   -Wl,-headerpad_max_install_names, and -Wl,-rpath entries for
   <prefix>/lib and <folly prefix>/lib so @rpath/libthrift_python_cpp and
   @rpath/libfolly* resolve without DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH.

5. thrift_python_cpp link: ld64 has neither --whole-archive nor
   --allow-multiple-definition. On APPLE the archives that must be embedded
   whole are linked through $<LINK_LIBRARY:WHOLE_ARCHIVE,...> (-force_load),
   while rpcmetadata and thriftmetadata are linked on demand: force-loading
   them duplicates generated symbols already provided by
   thriftcpp2/thriftprotocol (TJSONProtocol etc.), which ld64 rejects.
   The dylib is also linked with -undefined dynamic_lookup (Python symbols
   come from the interpreter) and -headerpad_max_install_names.

6. Wheel install: on APPLE the PLAIN wheel from dist/ is installed to
   share/thrift/wheels; auditwheel (Linux) is skipped. Its darwin analogue,
   delocate, vendors libfolly/gflags/glog/libpython copies into the wheel that
   dual-register against the folly package's copies at import time; one
   shared set of dylibs found through the rpaths above is the only sound
   layout.

Hunks in thrift/lib/setup.py:

7. --libpython parsing handles libpython3.X.dylib (was hardcoded to strip
   ".so"), and on darwin libpython is not linked at all (dynamic_lookup).

8. aio and unwind are Linux-only link libraries.

9. The $ORIGIN rpaths (auditwheel layout) are Linux-only.

10. The thrift.python.streaming.sink extension compiles SinkImpl.cpp (hunk 3).


(A) thrift/lib/cpp2/server/IOWorkerContext.h — register the per-IO-thread
reply queue with startConsuming() instead of startConsumingInternal().
Stock libevent's event_base_loop(EVLOOP_ONCE) only returns after a
NON-internal callback ran, so an internal-only wakeup (the reply queue's
pipe) never returns control to folly::EventBase::loopMain, folly's loop
callbacks never run, the queue is never re-armed, producers see
push()==false and stop signalling, and replies wait for an unrelated
event on that IO thread. Symptom on an idle EdenFS daemon on macOS: every
Thrift reply delayed 5-7s (handler ran in <2ms), which broke the CLI's
3s health check. Not darwin-specific in principle; masked on busy servers.
Reproduced standalone with folly alone (internal queue + loopForever).

diff --git a/thrift/lib/cpp2/server/IOWorkerContext.h b/thrift/lib/cpp2/server/IOWorkerContext.h
index ff826afbd9..3af3d8c616 100644
--- a/thrift/lib/cpp2/server/IOWorkerContext.h
+++ b/thrift/lib/cpp2/server/IOWorkerContext.h
@@ -63,7 +63,20 @@ class IOWorkerContext {
           auto aliveLocked = alive->rlock();
           if (*aliveLocked) {
             // IOWorkerContext is still alive and so is replyQueue_
-            queue->startConsumingInternal(&evb);
+            //
+            // Register the reply queue as a *normal* (non-internal) libevent
+            // event. With stock libevent, event_base_loop(EVLOOP_ONCE) only
+            // returns to the caller once a non-internal callback has run
+            // (event_process_active_single_queue skips EVLIST_INTERNAL when
+            // counting), so an internal-only wakeup never lets folly run its
+            // loop callbacks, the queue is never re-armed (that happens in
+            // EventBaseAtomicNotificationQueue::runLoopCallback), producers
+            // then see push() == false and stop signalling, and replies sit
+            // in the queue until an unrelated event wakes the IO thread. On
+            // an otherwise idle server (e.g. EdenFS on macOS answering one
+            // CLI request at a time) every reply was delayed 5-7s. IO threads
+            // run loopForever(), so keeping the loop alive is harmless.
+            queue->startConsuming(&evb);
           }
         });
   }
